-- title:
--   Constants of the Drinfeld function field are the base field
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number assumed prime, and let $k$ be a field carrying an algebra structure over the field $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$ of $q^2$ elements. Write $A =$ `CoordRing q k` for the quotient of the polynomial ring $k[X_0,X_1]$ in two variables by the principal ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`, and assume $A$ is a domain; let $F =$ `drinfeldFunctionField q k` be its fraction field. The theorem asserts two statements simultaneously. First, `ConstantsAreBase k F` holds: the Riemann–Roch space attached to the zero divisor in the group $\mathrm{Place}(k,F) \to_{\mathrm{f}} \mathbb{Z}$ of finitely supported integer-valued functions on the places of $F$ over $k$ — that is, the space of elements of $F$ with no pole at any place of $F/k$ — coincides with the range of the $k$-linear structure map $k \to F$. Second, every $y \in F$ that is algebraic over $k$ already lies in the range of the algebra map $k \to F$; equivalently, $F$ contains no elements algebraic over $k$ beyond the constants.
--
--   This is the statement that $k$ is the full field of constants of the Drinfeld curve's function field, in the two shapes needed later: the adelic (Riemann–Roch space of the zero divisor) and the algebraic (integral closure of $k$ in $F$) formulations. It feeds the analysis of the degree-zero divisor class group of the curve under base change and the vanishing results for intertwining maps attached to cuspidal types.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_DrinfeldCurve_constantsAreBase_drinfeldFunctionField.lean

